{"subjectId":"sql","id":"non-equi-joins","title":"Non-Equi Joins","group":"Joins & Set Operations","order":29,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A join condition doesn't have to be <code>=</code>. Ranges, inequalities and <code>BETWEEN</code> joins match rows by <em>intervals</em> — salary bands, price tiers, date validity periods.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE salary_grades (grade TEXT PRIMARY KEY, min_salary INTEGER, max_salary INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO salary_grades VALUES\n    ('G1', 0, 74999), ('G2', 75000, 99999), ('G3', 100000, 139999), ('G4', 140000, 999999);\n\n        SELECT * FROM salary_grades ORDER BY grade;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>grade | min_salary | max_salary\n------+------------+-----------\nG1    | 0          | 74999\nG2    | 75000      | 99999\nG3    | 100000     | 139999\nG4    | 140000     | 999999</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i>Joining on a range</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name, e.salary, g.grade\nFROM employees e\nJOIN salary_grades g ON e.salary BETWEEN g.min_salary AND g.max_salary\nORDER BY e.salary DESC, e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary | grade\n-------+--------+------\nAsha   | 150000 | G4\nRavi   | 120000 | G3\nPriya  | 110000 | G3\nMeera  | 95000  | G2\nKaran  | 95000  | G2\nSara   | 85000  | G2\nVikram | 80000  | G2\nNeha   | 72000  | G1\nArjun  | 70000  | G1\nDev    | 60000  | G1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-line\"></i>Inequality joins: running comparisons</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- for each employee, how many colleagues earn strictly more (their 0-based rank)\nSELECT e.name, e.salary, COUNT(h.emp_id) AS earn_more\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN employees h ON h.salary &gt; e.salary\nGROUP BY e.emp_id, e.name, e.salary\nORDER BY earn_more, e.emp_id\nLIMIT 5;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | salary | earn_more\n------+--------+----------\nAsha  | 150000 | 0\nRavi  | 120000 | 1\nPriya | 110000 | 2\nMeera | 95000  | 3\nKaran | 95000  | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- repeat orders: placed within 30 days after an earlier order by the same customer\nSELECT a.customer_id, a.order_id AS earlier, b.order_id AS later,\n       julianday(b.order_date) - julianday(a.order_date) AS gap_days\nFROM orders a\nJOIN orders b ON b.customer_id = a.customer_id\n             AND b.order_date &gt;  a.order_date\n             AND b.order_date &lt;= date(a.order_date, '+30 days')\nORDER BY a.customer_id, a.order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | earlier | later | gap_days\n------------+---------+-------+---------\n1           | 101     | 103   | 29.0\n3           | 104     | 107   | 23.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Range and inequality joins can explode in size (O(n²) pairs) and can't use hash joins. Make sure ranges don't overlap (or you get duplicates), index the range columns, and prefer window functions for ranking/running questions.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a non-equi join?</summary><div><p>A join whose condition uses an operator other than <code>=</code> — <code>BETWEEN</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;&gt;</code>. Typical: mapping a value to a band (<code>salary BETWEEN min AND max</code>), finding overlapping date ranges, or pairing events that occurred within some interval.</p></div></details></section>"}